<article class="enc-article">
<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Abu Inan Faris</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1329">1329</a>–<a href="/years/1358">1358</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a> · Of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinid dynasty</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Abu Inan <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fari">Faris</a> was sultan of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinid dynasty</a> of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/morocco">Morocco</a> from 1348 to 1358. He seized power from his father Abu al-Hasan, conquered the Zayyanid kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> in 1352 and briefly took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> in 1357. He built the Bou Inania <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meknes">Meknes</a> and commissioned the account of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>'s travels. He was strangled by his <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> in 1358.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/abu-inan-faris.jpg" alt="Abu Inan Faris" width="320" height="157" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: CNG · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Abu_Inan_coin.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Reign</h2><p>His father, Abu al-Hasan, had taken <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> in 1337 and annexed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ifriqiya">Ifriqiya</a> in 1347, briefly reuniting the Maghrib as it had been under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/almohad-caliphate">Almohads</a>. When the Arab tribes revolted and defeated Abu al-Hasan's army near <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kairouan">Kairouan</a> in April 1348, Abu Inan, then governor of Tlemcen, returned to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a>, declared himself sultan and took the title <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/commander-of-the-faithful">Amir al-Mu'minin</a>, commander of the believers. Abu al-Hasan, forced to come back from Ifriqiya by sea, failed to retake Tlemcen, was defeated by his son and died in May 1351.</p><p>After recapturing <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> in 1352, Abu Inan reconquered the central <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/maghreb">Maghreb</a> and took <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bejaia">Béjaïa</a> in 1353. The Nasrid rulers of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/granada">Granada</a>, fearing he would invade them once he held the whole Maghreb, backed a rebellion by his brother Abul Fadl, who had briefly governed <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a>; Sultan Abul-Hajjaj of Granada hired ships from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kingdom-of-castile">Castile</a> to carry the rebels to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/sousse">Sousse</a>, where the rising was short-lived. Abu Inan took Tunis in 1357, but the intrigues of his vizier <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fari">Faris</a> bin Maymum forced him to withdraw from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunisia">Tunisia</a> the same year.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Death and legacy</h2><p>Abu Inan fell ill in November 1357. His vizier Hasan bin Umar al-Fududi, at odds with the heir apparent Abu Zayyan Muhammad, named the sultan's young son Abu Bakr Sa'id as successor instead. When Abu Inan began to recover, the vizier, fearing he would be punished for the nomination, had him strangled on 10 January 1358.</p><p>The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/black-death">Black Death</a> and the rebellions of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tlemcen">Tlemcen</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tunis">Tunis</a> under his rule marked the beginning of the decline of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/marinid-dynasty">Marinids</a>, who proved unable to drive back the Portuguese and Spaniards; these began to settle on the North African coast under the Wattasids, who succeeded the Marinids. Abu Inan had his secretary Ibn Juzayy record <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ibn-battuta">Ibn Battuta</a>'s 29 years of travel, a rihla completed in December 1355. He built <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/madrasa">madrasas</a> in <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/meknes">Meknes</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/fez">Fez</a> in 1350 and another in Fez in 1357, and Mont Bouenan, south of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/tetouan">Tetouan</a>, was named after him.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Abu Inan Faris” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>In 1348 Abu al-Hasan, who had lost Tunisia to the Arab revolt, was deposed by his son <b>Abu Inan Faris</b>, and the Black Death and the rebellions of Tlemcen and Tunis marked the turn; in 1358 Abu Inan was strangled by his own vizier.</blockquote><a href="/empire/marinid-sultanate">Marinid Sultanate · Decline &amp; Fall</a></li></ul></section>
<section class="enc-where enc-born"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Born and died</h2><p class="enc-born-p">Born in Fez, in what is now <a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a>. Died in Fez, in what is now <a href="/countries/morocco">Morocco</a>. <span class="enc-born-src">Places as Wikidata records them.</span></p><figure class="st-locator enc-loc"><a href="/atlas?at=34.04,-5&amp;year=1347" aria-label="Fez on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="4423 930 900 540" role="img" aria-label="Map: the birthplace of Abu Inan Faris"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M4873 1200h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M4873 1200h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks the birthplace. <a href="/atlas?at=34.04,-5&amp;year=1347">What was here in 1347? &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></section>
